# ===========================================================================
# CECODES - Huella de Carbono
#
# Local development:  copy to `.env.local`
# Docker deployment:  copy to `.env`      (docker-compose.yml reads `.env`)
#
# Every variable below already has a working default (see docker-compose.yml), so this file
# exists only to override them. The one exception is a non-container deployment (running the
# built app directly with `node server.js`, no compose): there, DATABASE_URL is the one thing you
# must set, because there is no db service to default it to.
#
# A note on the quotes below, learned the hard way. `docker compose` strips surrounding quotes
# from an env file, so the values here work as written. `docker run --env-file` does NOT: it
# passes "postgresql://..." through with the quote characters still in the string, and the
# connection then fails with nothing in the message to suggest why. If you ever run a container
# by hand rather than through compose, drop the quotes.
# ===========================================================================


# ===========================================================================
# REQUIRED - the app refuses to start without this
#
# src/instrumentation.ts validates DATABASE_URL at boot; nothing else in the runtime schema is
# mandatory. A missing DATABASE_URL fails immediately naming the variable, rather than a 500 on
# the first request. Under docker compose it already defaults to the db service below, so this is
# the one thing worth setting by hand for a deployment that is NOT running that container, e.g.
# a plain `node server.js` pointed at a database you manage yourself.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Database ---
#
# Two URLs for one database. The app runs many short-lived queries over DATABASE_URL; Prisma
# migrations need a session, not a pooled connection, so they use DIRECT_URL. Point either at a
# pooler (pgbouncer, Supavisor) or at plain Postgres directly; nothing here assumes one provider.
# Under docker compose, both default to the db service and need no override.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://cecodes:cecodes-local-dev@localhost:5432/cecodes"
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://cecodes:cecodes-local-dev@localhost:5432/cecodes"

# --- The database container itself (used only when db is the one running) ---
#
# docker-compose.yml builds DATABASE_URL's default from these three, so changing one here changes
# the other automatically; there is no separate place to keep them in step.
POSTGRES_USER="cecodes"
POSTGRES_PASSWORD="cecodes-local-dev"
POSTGRES_DB="cecodes"


# ===========================================================================
# REQUIRED to initialize the database - scripts/init-db.ts validates both before touching it
# ===========================================================================

# --- The first admin, created idempotently by prisma/seed.ts ---
#
# This is the ONLY way into a fresh system: self-serve registration is off
# (src/lib/feature-flags.ts, FEATURE_SELF_ONBOARDING = false).
#
# ADMIN_EMAIL has a working default under docker compose (admin@cecodes.local, see
# docker-compose.yml), so set your own here mainly to avoid keeping the placeholder. ADMIN_PASSWORD
# has NO default anywhere, deliberately: a shipped default password is a vulnerability, not a
# convenience. Leaving it unset FAILS initialization rather than quietly finishing with no account.
# Re-running the seed keeps the password in sync with this file.
ADMIN_EMAIL="admin@example.com"
ADMIN_PASSWORD="<strong-password-min-12-chars>"


# ===========================================================================
# OPTIONAL - sensible defaults, set only if you need them
# ===========================================================================

# Public hostname, used only by the optional TLS proxy:
#   docker compose --profile edge up -d
# DNS must already point at this server. Unset means http://localhost.
# DOMAIN="huella.example.org"

# Port the app listens on inside its container. Rarely worth changing; the
# published binding lives in docker-compose.yml.
# PORT="3000"

# Public origin used to build the links in password-reset and confirmation
# emails. It is pinned in configuration because the alternative is reading the
# request's Host header, which an attacker controls: a crafted request would
# have this app mail a real user a real token pointing at another site.
#
# Usually you do not need this. DOMAIN above already answers the question, and
# SITE_URL is only for a deployment whose public address differs from it. With
# neither set, emailed links are built in development from the request host and
# in production not at all, so a reset mail fails visibly instead of pointing
# somewhere unintended.
#
# It must be a full URL including the scheme, not a bare hostname like DOMAIN
# above. The app checks that at boot and refuses to start on anything else.
# Until it did, a hostname here was silently discarded: the override you set on
# purpose was ignored, and the links fell back to DOMAIN, or to nowhere when
# DOMAIN was unset too.
# SITE_URL="https://huella.example.org"

# --- Mail ---
#
# MAIL_TRANSPORT chooses how password-reset mail is sent:
#
#   smtp    Talk to an SMTP server directly. The default, and what docker-compose.yml points at
#           Mailpit for, so a reset link works out of the box with no provider account. Read the
#           messages it caught at http://127.0.0.1:8025.
#   resend  Use the Resend API instead. Set RESEND_API_KEY and MAIL_FROM below when you do.
#
# MAIL_TRANSPORT="smtp"
# SMTP_HOST="mailpit"
# SMTP_PORT="1025"
# SMTP_USER=""
# SMTP_PASSWORD=""

# Password-reset email, sent through Resend (https://resend.com), for a production deployment
# that does not want to run its own SMTP relay.
#
# Both or neither, and the app refuses to start on one without the other: a key
# with no From address cannot address a message, and a From address with no key
# cannot send one.
#
# MAIL_FROM is a From header, so either a bare address or "Name <address>".
# The domain has to be one you verified with Resend.
# RESEND_API_KEY="<resend-api-key>"
# MAIL_FROM="CECODES <no-reply@huella.example.org>"


# ===========================================================================
# DEVELOPMENT ONLY - must never be set on a deployed server
# ===========================================================================

# Demo companies and sample activity data (prisma/seed-demo.ts, run with
# `bun run db:seed:demo`).
#
# There is ONE shared production database, so this variable is the only thing
# standing between that script and real client data. scripts/init-db.ts
# refuses to run at all when it is "true", so an automated deployment cannot
# create demo tenants by accident.
DEMO_SEED_ALLOWED="false"
DEMO_PASSWORD="<demo-password>"

# Skips admin creation during `bun run db:seed`. Only for working against a
# database whose admin already exists. Never set this in a deployment: it
# turns the fail-loud check above back into a silent skip.
# SEED_SKIP_ADMIN="false"
